1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,844
You'll hear that exact line defending the next feature too.

2
00:00:03,844 --> 00:00:08,819
Here's the question it never answers: is a summariser actually the same conversation

3
00:00:08,819 --> 00:00:11,306
as an agent that books refunds on its own?

4
00:00:11,306 --> 00:00:14,445
Not even close, even when the same model powers both.

5
00:00:14,445 --> 00:00:19,005
By now you've got the mechanism, the frontier, and why hallucination happens.

6
00:00:19,005 --> 00:00:22,735
Here's where that turns into something you can use in the room.

7
00:00:22,735 --> 00:00:27,532
Almost every AI feature anyone builds falls into one of seven archetypes, and the

8
00:00:27,532 --> 00:00:32,507
archetype tells you most of what you need to know about how hard this is going to be

9
00:00:32,507 --> 00:00:35,882
to ship safely, before you've written a line of the spec.

10
00:00:35,882 --> 00:00:40,324
The mistake I see constantly is treating "should we use AI for this" as one

11
00:00:40,324 --> 00:00:41,094
conversation.

12
00:00:41,094 --> 00:00:46,068
It's actually seven different conversations wearing the same trenchcoat, and they do

13
00:00:46,068 --> 00:00:47,490
not carry the same risk.

14
00:00:47,490 --> 00:00:51,517
Watch for that defence, in exactly that shape, in your own meetings.

15
00:00:52,067 --> 00:00:56,314
Here are the seven, roughly ordered by how directly they touch the user.

16
00:00:56,314 --> 00:01:00,679
Summarise and extract both take real text you already have and reshape it,

17
00:01:00,679 --> 00:01:03,274
condensing or pulling structured fields out.

18
00:01:03,274 --> 00:01:08,111
Classify sorts something into a bucket you already defined, like routing a ticket.

19
00:01:08,111 --> 00:01:12,948
Draft writes new words from a prompt, for a human to review before anything ships.

20
00:01:12,948 --> 00:01:16,842
Search ranks existing documents so a human picks from the results.

21
00:01:16,842 --> 00:01:20,145
Answer skips that step and states a conclusion directly.

22
00:01:20,145 --> 00:01:24,982
And act is the one that actually does something in the world, booking, cancelling,

23
00:01:24,982 --> 00:01:28,639
updating, on the user's behalf, with no draft step in between.

24
00:01:28,639 --> 00:01:33,181
Notice the drift as you go down that list: less and less human review sitting

25
00:01:33,181 --> 00:01:35,423
between the model and the consequence.

26
00:01:35,423 --> 00:01:40,260
A single product often contains several of these at once, and that's normal, not a

27
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:40,791
red flag.

28
00:01:40,791 --> 00:01:45,392
A support tool might summarise the thread, extract the account number, draft a

29
00:01:45,392 --> 00:01:48,931
reply, and route the ticket, four archetypes in one feature.

30
00:01:48,931 --> 00:01:53,827
Name each piece separately, because they don't share a risk level just because they

31
00:01:53,827 --> 00:01:54,476
share a UI.

32
00:01:55,026 --> 00:01:57,975
Here's the pattern the last slide was building to.

33
00:01:57,975 --> 00:02:02,870
Same seven archetypes, ordered by how much evaluation rigour they genuinely demand.

34
00:02:02,870 --> 00:02:07,707
Summarise and extract sit low, because you're reshaping text that's right there in

35
00:02:07,707 --> 00:02:11,836
the prompt, exactly where the mechanism from lecture one is strongest.

36
00:02:11,836 --> 00:02:15,433
A spot check and a light golden set will catch most problems.

37
00:02:15,433 --> 00:02:18,441
Answer and act sit high, and for different reasons.

38
00:02:18,441 --> 00:02:23,278
Answer skips the human review step search kept, so a wrong answer reaches the user

39
00:02:23,278 --> 00:02:23,809
directly.

40
00:02:23,809 --> 00:02:28,291
Act compounds that: it's not just wrong, it's wrong and it already happened.

41
00:02:28,291 --> 00:02:33,246
Those two are where you want the entire Section four toolkit, not the light version.

42
00:02:33,246 --> 00:02:38,200
Draft and search sit in the middle for a genuinely different reason than either end.

43
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:43,154
They're not risky because the model is unreliable on that task, they're risky if the

44
00:02:43,154 --> 00:02:46,398
human review step gets skipped under deadline pressure.

45
00:02:46,398 --> 00:02:50,822
The archetype is only as safe as the review step someone swore they'd keep.

46
00:02:51,372 --> 00:02:56,023
Say the honest caveat, because a chart like that invites the wrong conclusion if

47
00:02:56,023 --> 00:02:57,127
you're not careful.

48
00:02:57,127 --> 00:02:59,802
This is not a verdict on any specific feature.

49
00:02:59,802 --> 00:03:01,429
It's a prediction of effort.

50
00:03:01,429 --> 00:03:06,080
A well-evaluated "act" feature, with the reliability maths from Section six done

51
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:08,231
properly, can absolutely ship safely.

52
00:03:08,231 --> 00:03:12,940
A "summarise" feature that skipped Section four entirely can still embarrass you,

53
00:03:12,940 --> 00:03:16,254
just with lower odds and probably a smaller blast radius.

54
00:03:16,254 --> 00:03:20,265
The chart tells you how much you owe the problem before you trust it.

55
00:03:20,265 --> 00:03:22,126
It doesn't do the owing for you.

56
00:03:22,126 --> 00:03:26,893
Use it to argue about budget and timeline honestly, not to argue that a feature is

57
00:03:26,893 --> 00:03:29,858
safe because of what category it happens to sit in.

58
00:03:29,858 --> 00:03:34,276
"It's just a summariser" has talked more than one team out of eval work they

59
00:03:34,276 --> 00:03:35,206
actually needed.

60
00:03:35,756 --> 00:03:39,179
Notice the definition is about consequence, not appearance.

61
00:03:39,179 --> 00:03:43,820
Two features can look identical in the UI, a chat window, a text box, and sit at

62
00:03:43,820 --> 00:03:48,461
completely different points on that chart, because one drafts for review and the

63
00:03:48,461 --> 00:03:49,622
other acts directly.

64
00:03:49,622 --> 00:03:51,536
The exercise is blunt on purpose.

65
00:03:51,536 --> 00:03:53,915
Say your feature's archetype in one word.

66
00:03:53,915 --> 00:03:58,614
If nothing on that list fits cleanly, or more than one does at once, that's worth

67
00:03:58,614 --> 00:04:03,313
sitting with before you write a single acceptance criterion, because the spec you

68
00:04:03,313 --> 00:04:06,852
write depends entirely on which one you're actually building.

69
00:04:06,852 --> 00:04:09,405
Do this before Section three, not during it.

70
00:04:09,405 --> 00:04:14,220
Walking into a PRD already knowing your archetype is the difference between writing

71
00:04:14,220 --> 00:04:19,035
the spec once and rewriting it after someone asks a question you hadn't considered.

72
00:04:19,585 --> 00:04:21,262
One thing before you move on.

73
00:04:21,262 --> 00:04:24,846
Name your feature's archetype, one word, out loud or on paper.

74
00:04:24,846 --> 00:04:29,645
Then be honest about whether the evaluation work you've actually done matches where

75
00:04:29,645 --> 00:04:33,114
that archetype sits on the chart, not where you wish it sat.

76
00:04:33,114 --> 00:04:37,566
If you're building several archetypes in one product, do this once per piece.

77
00:04:37,566 --> 00:04:42,017
A single honest sentence, "we've built a search feature's worth of rigour for

78
00:04:42,017 --> 00:04:46,874
something that actually answers," is worth more than the whole rest of this lecture.

79
00:04:46,874 --> 00:04:51,441
Next lecture is the checklist for the question underneath all of this: when the

80
00:04:51,441 --> 00:04:56,297
honest answer, for all seven archetypes, is that you shouldn't build this with AI at

81
00:04:56,297 --> 00:04:56,529
all.
